ntdll: system calls write into the emulator's write trap, as the Windows kernel does.

FEX makes a read-write-execute page that holds translated code read-execute, so that a write into it faults into
its tracking of self-modifying code. A write by the program itself reaches FEX that way, but a system call that
writes into such a page for the program faulted on the Unix side, in virtual_handle_fault, and returned
STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION. Windows has no such trap, and the same call succeeds there. FEX's own
BTCpu64NotifyReadFile names the case ("FEX could have write-protected the memory region when a JIT executed the
code, and once the NtReadFile tries returning data, it faults. This requires coordination between Wine and FEX").

Football Manager 27 (Game Pass) stopped at "Loading game database": its copy protection asked for the SMBIOS table
with NtQuerySystemInformation(SystemFirmwareTableInformation) into a buffer on a page next to code it had run,
the call returned STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION after a fault at the buffer's TableBufferLength, and the protection then
read an address it had computed from the missing answer (an unhandled access violation in its own code).

The write takes the same steps as a write by the program, in FEX's order: the translations of the page go, the
page opens, and the translations go again (a translation made in between traps the page again).

- ntdll's ARM64EC side marks the pages the emulator traps: after a successful NtProtectVirtualMemory on the
  current process, each 4 KB page that the new protection leaves not writable, while GuestProtection (0043) says
  the program made it writable, goes to the Unix side through a private NtSetInformationVirtualMemory class
  (0x4842). The Unix side keeps the mark in a free page flag (VPROT_HB_EMU_TRAP), on committed pages only. Any
  other protection change and a decommit clear it, and region queries ignore it, so a program sees the same
  regions as before.
- A write fault from inside a system call (is_inside_syscall) into a marked page returns STATUS_HB_EMULATOR_TRAP
  to the signal handler. The handler saves the faulting state below the stack pointer and sends the thread to a
  trampoline. The trampoline calls KeUserModeCallback (id 0x4846). KiUserCallbackDispatcher hands that id to
  hb_emulator_trap_callback: BTCpu64NotifyMemoryDirty, open the page (class 0x4845), BTCpu64NotifyMemoryDirty.
  Then brk #0x4847 gives the saved state back, and the write runs again. The page stays open until FEX
  translates code from it again. If the callback cannot run, the trampoline opens the page without FEX.
- FEX calls NtProtectVirtualMemory while it holds its lock for code invalidation, and the callback takes that
  lock. So the callback runs during the call only when the thread holds no Unix lock: mutex_lock and mutex_unlock
  count the locks of each thread, and server_enter_uninterrupted_section takes its lock through them. Under a
  lock (for example virtual_mutex in NtQueryVirtualMemory, NtGetWriteWatch and NtFreeVirtualMemory, or dir_mutex
  in NtQueryDirectoryFile) the page opens on the Unix side and is recorded for the thread, and a flag in the
  syscall frame (RESTORE_FLAGS_HB_NOTIFY) makes the dispatcher run the same callback for those pages when the call
  returns, with no lock held. A call without such a write pays nothing. Until the call returns, another thread
  can run old code from such a page, as with BTCpu64NotifyReadFile.
- Not covered: data that wineserver writes straight into a trapped page (a server reply such as NtQueryObject
  with ObjectNameInformation, NtQueryInformationProcess with ProcessImageFileName, or a registry query) fails in
  read_reply_data with EFAULT and still ends the thread. Asynchronous I/O that completes later is not covered
  either.
- macOS only (the trampoline uses Darwin's mcontext); other hosts keep the old result.

tests/x64-fidelity/syscallwrite (M5 Pro, macOS 27.2), 39 checks: Rosetta 2 39 of 39. r5, without this patch: 23 of
the first 34, then the lock check hangs. With this patch: 39 of 39 in 10 of 10 runs. Without the lock count, the
lock check hung in 5 of 5 runs; with a notice only after NtQueryVirtualMemory, writewatch.no-flush ran old code.
VirtualQuery in a loop: 1,173,000 calls a second on r5, 1,165,000 with this patch.

